The kinetic energy is $E_\text{k} = \tfrac12 mv^2$. Convert first: $$
\begin{aligned}
&72\,\text{km/h} \\
&= 72 \cdot \dfrac{1000\,\text{m}}{3600\,\text{s}} \\
&= 20\,\text{m/s}
\end{aligned}
$$, so $$
\begin{aligned}
E_\text{k} &= \tfrac12 \cdot 1500\,\text{kg} \cdot \left(20\,\tfrac{\text{m}}{\text{s}}\right)^2 \\
&= 300\,000\,\text{J} \\
&= 0.30\,\text{MJ}
\end{aligned}
$$.

Momentum is conserved: $m_1 v_1 = (m_1 + m_2)\,v'$, so $v' = \dfrac{m_1 v_1}{m_1 + m_2}$. Then $$
\begin{aligned}
&2.0\,\text{kg} \cdot 3.0\,\tfrac{\text{m}}{\text{s}} \\
&= (2.0\,\text{kg} + 1.0\,\text{kg})\,v'
\end{aligned}
$$, so $$
v' = \dfrac{6.0\,\text{kg} \cdot \text{m/s}}{3.0\,\text{kg}} = 2.0\,\text{m/s}
$$. The kinetic energy decreases from $9.0\,\text{J}$ to $6.0\,\text{J}$.
